module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.surface_shorthand_preempts_resolved_identity

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data surface_shorthand_preempts_resolved_identity: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "surface_shorthand_preempts_resolved_identity" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**surface shorthand preempts resolved identity** (a semantic consumer dispatches on a raw lexeme after resolution has already named its declaration, so a declared constructor is reinterpreted as a host sentinel or primitive.",

    "Recognition rule: once a node carries a binding/declaration identity, value construction must be total over that identity; spelling may be interpreted only on an explicitly unresolved shorthand arm.",

    "Specimen gunbc#9741: bare and imported `None` became host `Null` while qualified `Diagnostics.None {}` became a `Variant`; a single-frame comparison hid the fork.",

    "This is not hollow alias: the binding is grounded and present, then discarded downstream.",

    "It is also separate from the per-frame symbol-ordinal defect: that defect corrupted lexeme identity while crossing frames, whereas this one ignores an identity already supplied at one construction site.",

    "SECOND SPECIMEN (2026-09-11, v1_interpreter eval_var's variable slow path, gunbc#11065). The evaluator held the resolved node from the file-aware resolver and still took the item KIND from item_registry, a projection keyed by bare SPELLING that keeps one homonym per spelling in hash-seed order. One fact, two authorities: a data reference to extdeps.vendor.gigabyte's gigabyte was classified by std.measure's fn gigabyte on some processes and evaluated as a function, and a field read refused with cannot access field 'legal_name' on Fn. Found at: mitigatable where the wrong kind reached a typed refusal, and silent wrongness wherever a mis-kinded value flows on without one. The instrument that exposed and discharges it is an N-run comparison across per-process hash seeds on one probe, a control binary built from the base against the repaired binary, recorded on gunbc#11065; test.claim.data_reference_kind_from_resolved_node is only a probabilistic regression control for it, never its coverage.",

    "WHAT THAT REPAIR CLOSES AND WHAT IT LEAVES, per path, because the rung is the minimum across them -- enumerated over every item_registry read in v1_interpreter. CLOSED: eval_var now takes the kind from item_kind of the resolved node, the function that computed the registry's kind, so its kind and node can no longer disagree. KIND COLLAPSED, SELECTION NOT: eval_data_item_value, eval_data_initializer_values and resolve_published_mock_keys classify the node they evaluate (resolve_published_mock_keys for UNIFORMITY, not to fix a live instance: on main no data declaration typed PublishedMockCase shares its name with any other data, fn, type, service or resource declaration, so that site is not reachable by the data/fn homonym today; a same-spelled COPRODUCT ARM was NOT checked, and that channel -- an arm is not a local binding under its own spelling, so it lands in the pool -- is exactly the one binding_chosen_by_pool_membership_rather_than_by_the_declared_rule is about, so this is unchecked there, not clear), but they are asked by bare name with no referring file and still SELECT that node through the shared bare slot, whose owner for a colliding spelling is chosen by walk order rather than by declaration, so they can still evaluate or decline the wrong declaration. GUARDED: build_initial_env still reads the registry kind but skips every name with more than one declaration, so its registry entry and slot are one declaration. SAME LAW, DIFFERENT MECHANISM, LEFT OPEN: try_witness_evaluation_dispatch and the two v2 collection grounding gates hold a resolved node yet ask the bare registry which MODULE it belongs to, so a homonym can attribute the node to another module and misroute a primitive dispatch; closing them needs node-to-module attribution, not item_kind. OUT OF CLASS: resolution_fingerprint observes the projection itself rather than reading what a name denotes. The class therefore stays mitigatable on every path but eval_var.",

    "NEXT RUNG FOR THIS SPECIMEN, a capability and not an artifact: an ambiguous bare name that reaches the shared slot is REFUSED, landed together with the renames that make the corpus pass it and never with an allow-list, so a reference with no declaration-aware answer has no evaluation at all. That makes the ambiguity unwritable rather than unlikely to be observed, and it is the step the existing trigger below names for evaluator construction in general.",

    "THIRD SPECIMEN (2026-09-19, gunbc#11603, review 68098/68166). A bare `Https` or `Http`, imported by name from extdeps.uri, evaluates to NoSuchVariable in any closure that also reaches gunbc.cloudflare.r2_origin, while the same literal evaluates in a minimal module; v2.extdeps.coordination CoordinationEffectKind also declares an `Http` variant, and the resolved import is discarded for a bare-spelling lookup that the colliding family defeats. The module-qualified `extdeps.uri.Https` evaluates correctly in the same closure, which is the discriminator and the route #11603 now uses (the interim uri_scheme_is_https predicate was deleted). Still open: the bare imported form. Reproduce with `gunbc run --entry dag/test/claim/cloudflare_boot_origin_witness_test.dag --function readback_refuses_plain_http` after replacing the qualified reference in boot_origin.dag with the bare imported one.",

    "FOURTH SPECIMEN (2026-09-24, gunbc#12185, found by side-chat review). The service-receiver stamp joins on the bare NAME, not the owning declaration: v1.compiler.infer infer_var_binding_kind stamps ServiceValueBinding when the service registry holds the local's name and the local's type is the nominal reference the scope fold inserted (local_is_service_root_binding), without comparing that reference's owner module to the registry row's. gunbc.runner_guest_image calls `Filesystem.Write(...)` and reads `.success`/`.error`, which is extdeps.filesystem.filesystem_io's service Filesystem. When #12185 first named a source for that bare read, it imported std.resources { Filesystem } (a resource whose capability is lowercase `write` with output `written`), and the module still type-checked and emitted, because the registry row matched by name. The import was corrected to extdeps.filesystem.filesystem_io { Filesystem }, checked by reading the call: `.success` and `.error` exist only on that service's Write, not on the resource's `write`. The join itself is unrepaired. THE DISCRIMINATING RED IS UNAUTHORABLE ON EVERY ROUTE TODAY: on the seed, the name-only join is the defect, so a mutant import back to std.resources type-checks and emits green; on v2, v2.extdeps.languages.dag has no resource production (the resource shape refuses at parse) and v2.compiler.namespace_graft places only fn/data/type declarations in the containment tree, so a service receiver has no declaring path to assert. Next rung, two capabilities, either of which makes the red authorable: (a) the seed's service stamp requires the local's resolved declaration and the registry row to be the SAME declaration (owner module plus name), so a two-declaration fixture refuses the mutant import at the call; (b) v2 grafts service declarations into the containment tree and gains a resource production, so the receiver's declaring path is assertable.",

    "FOURTH SPECIMEN CLIMBED (2026-09-24, node adhoc-a42f3901-8f2). The seed's service registry was the closure-wide census keyed by leaf name, and every bare-declared service's root was seeded as a local of every module, so the join could not see owners. A bare-declared service is now in a module's scope only when the module declares it or imports its owner by that name (v1.compiler.infer census_service_in_scope, imported_service_in_scope), and a receiver that would have reached the old join through another module's same-named declaration refuses ServiceOwnerMismatch, typed and located at the receiver, with no fallback (service_owner_mismatch_refusal). Collisions are decided by POSITION, never by insertion order: two VALUES under one name refuse at every receiver, while a type or resource in a type or uses position does not compete with a service in receiver position. The census itself was a second instance of the class -- it held ONE service per spelling, so a second same-named service replaced the first before any ambiguity was judged -- and now holds every declaration of a name (v1.compiler.infer_env service_census_candidates); a module's candidates are collected from its declarations and ALL its imports, `import *` included, and decided only over the whole set: exactly one service is admitted, two or more services or a service beside a function or data value refuse as AmbiguousReference, and the bare-reference closure (cli_run census_single_service_owner) refuses a plural pull rather than picking one. Evidence: test.claim.service_owner_identity_witness (service import accepted with the service's output typed; resource-only import refuses at the receiver; both imported, the uses-typed resource and the service receiver each resolve; two same-named designed services refuse in either import order and through `import *`, either one alone is admitted while both exist, and service + resource + data refuses in either order). The dotted service roots (shell., git., github., ...) never reach this registry, and scoping their root locals is a separate, resolver-censused change.",

    "RESIDUAL, recorded and not fixed: one spelling meaning two declarations in one module is still a meaning fork (DESIGN 3), made unambiguous but not removed. gunbc.runner_microvm_slot_controller writes Filesystem for std.resources's resource in its uses clauses and for extdeps.filesystem.filesystem_io's service at its receivers. Trigger, a capability: qualified or aliased import references, so a module can spell std.resources.Filesystem and the filesystem_io service differently in source.",

    "Next-rung trigger: host literals and intentional primitive representations are typed parser/resolver constructions, and evaluator value construction is total over every resolved binding with no raw-spelling arm able to override it.)",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
